{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788959513,"nonce":"k67nU+Z6JeKwLS25JdFmSGSPrfQ=","message":"CODE LANDED, ACCEPTANCE UNDISCHARGED. ca626b3 on main, rebuilt in the commit after\nit. RimWorld was never launched and none of the 28 steps in\naccept/827c1bf-provenance.md has been run.\n\nTHREE FINDINGS THAT CHANGED THE IMPLEMENTATION, each verified by member name:\n\n1. Frame : Building, and Frame.Destroy calls base.Destroy(mode) — a non-virtual call\n   into a body Harmony patches, so both postfixes fire and every frame would have\n   been counted twice. The building hook now hands frames to the frame hook.\n2. Command_Action and Command_Toggle call base.ProcessInput(ev) FIRST and then act,\n   so the row precedes the effect — which is the right ordering and the reason the\n   base is the hook. But 6 of 23 vanilla overrides never call base\n   (Designator_Build, Designator_Install, Designator_Dropdown, Designator_Paint,\n   Designator_MechControlGroup, and one gizmo in Comp_AtmosphericHeater). Those arm a\n   cursor; USING the cursor is caught by the designator hook, so the gap is \"picking\n   a designator writes no gizmo row\", which acceptance step 18b measures rather than\n   assumes.\n3. TickManager.DoSingleTick is hooked directly, because since 1.8 the mod calls it\n   nowhere. A bracket around our own code would have labelled a starvation death\n   during a human's play window `human`. GameComponentTick is too late — it runs\n   after MapPostTick. Prefix plus FINALIZER rather than postfix, because\n   DoSingleTick leaves MapPreTick, MapPostTick and the tick lists outside its\n   try/catch.\n\nTHE CLOCK RECONCILIATION WAS REFUSED, AND THE REFUSAL IS RIGHT. The orchestrator\nasked whether `clock`'s existing by:external|mod should fold into this issue's four\nvalues, on the theory that `external` and `human` are the same fact. They are not.\nTimeDriver's own header already says `by` \"is NOT a claim about whose finger was on\nthe key. An `unpause` verb the agent itself sent reads `external`\" — so folding would\nassert a finger in a case that provably occurs. Instead the clock payload field is\nrenamed `drove` (values and meaning unchanged), the row-level `by` is the four-value\nprovenance, and since_last_look.outside[].by becomes .drove for the same reason.\nNothing in rwa/, cockpit/ or accept/ read either field.\n\nAnd a clock row from FrameStep does NOT get correct provenance from the\ndefault-to-human rule: it would read `human`, which is wrong on an `external` span\n(our observer wrote the row) and self-contradictory on a `mod` span. ClockEmit opens\nProvenance.Chore() explicitly, as four other standing observers already do — the\nalert scanner, the name-dialog sweep, boot, and unloaded.\n\nONE LIMIT, NOT APPROXIMATED. Verse/DiaOption.Activate() is hooked, but\nDialog_MessageBox has no funnel — three inline Widgets.ButtonText calls invoking\nbuttonAAction/buttonBAction/buttonCAction directly. It is NOT patched and NOT\napproximated: acceptance step 18 asks Dorian to press one and confirm no row\nappears, so the limit is measured rather than assumed.\n\nCOST, reasoned rather than measured: DoSingleTick is two thread-static writes plus\nHarmony's wrapper against a tick that is hundreds of microseconds on a 38-mod bench,\nzero allocation. The destroy hooks are reached only when one of those things actually\nends. The one OnGUI-path hook reads a thread-static first and a null second, and\nallocates nothing unless a click was consumed. The issue's timing bullet — measuring\nBuilding.Destroy across a 60,000-tick advance with a fire burning — is a measurement\nand remains Dorian's.\n\nFOR 975973e, THE SCREEN, WHICH DEPENDS ON THIS: `through_losses` is a new fourth\nadvance escape and `losses_rode_past` a new result field; the screen will want both,\nplus by/cmd on every row and the `drove` rename.\n\nVerified on the merged tree before the rebuild: s13-mod-surface --selftest 38/38,\nb1b3060-posture --selftest 26/26, rwa/selftest.sh 263/263. None of that asserts\nanything about the mod in front of a game.","files":null}]}